{"id":"workloads-compositepodgroup-api","corpus":"k8s","title":"CompositePodGroup API","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/compositepodgroup-api/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"CompositePodGroup API"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the CompositePodGroup feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"A CompositePodGroup is a runtime object that represents a non-leaf node in a multi-level workload hierarchy. While the Workload API defines static scheduling policy templates, CompositePodGroup and PodGroup objects are the runtime counterparts that carry policies and hierarchy references for a specific workload instance."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What is a CompositePodGroup?"},{"kind":"p","text":"The CompositePodGroup API resource is part of the scheduling.k8s.io/v1alpha3 API group. Your cluster must have that API group enabled, as well as the CompositePodGroup feature gate, before you can use this API."},{"kind":"p","text":"A CompositePodGroup represents a grouping of child groups (which can be CompositePodGroup or PodGroup objects). It carries scheduling policies, disruption modes, priority settings, and optional topology constraints that apply collectively across its child groups."},{"kind":"h2","text":"API structure"},{"kind":"p","text":"A CompositePodGroup consists of a spec that defines the desired scheduling behavior for its child groups, and a status subresource."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Scheduling policy"},{"kind":"p","text":"Each CompositePodGroup carries a scheduling policy (basic or gang) in spec.schedulingPolicy. When a workload controller creates a CompositePodGroup, this policy is copied from the Workload's CompositePodGroupTemplate at creation time."},{"kind":"p","text":"For a gang policy on a CompositePodGroup, the minGroupCount field specifies the minimum number of child groups that must be schedulable simultaneously:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Parent group reference"},{"kind":"p","text":"Non-root CompositePodGroup resources specify their parent group using spec.parentCompositePodGroupName. Root CompositePodGroup objects leave this field unset."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Workload reference"},{"kind":"p","text":"The spec.workloadRef field links the CompositePodGroup back to the CompositePodGroupTemplate in the Workload object it was derived from."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Status"},{"kind":"p","text":"The CompositePodGroup API schema includes a status subresource. In the alpha release, the status field is present in the API type, but kube-scheduler does not update or populate status conditions for CompositePodGroup objects. Status tracking for composite groups will be implemented in future releases."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Creating a CompositePodGroup"},{"kind":"p","text":"Workload controllers create CompositePodGroup objects automatically from Workload templates at runtime."},{"kind":"p","text":"The following manifest creates a root CompositePodGroup with a gang scheduling policy that requires at least 2 child groups to be schedulable simultaneously:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can inspect CompositePodGroup resources in your cluster:"},{"kind":"p","text":"To view details for a specific composite group:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"How it fits together"},{"kind":"p","text":"The relationship between controllers, Workloads, CompositePodGroups, PodGroups, and Pods follows this pattern:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The workload controller creates a Workload defining a tree of CompositePodGroupTemplates and leaf PodGroupTemplates."},{"kind":"li","text":"For each runtime instance, the controller creates a root CompositePodGroup, descendant CompositePodGroup objects, and leaf PodGroup objects in a top-down manner."},{"kind":"li","text":"The controller creates Pods that reference their leaf PodGroup via spec.schedulingGroup.podGroupName."},{"kind":"p","text":"The following example illustrates a complete manifest hierarchy for a two-level workload:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Workload acts as a long-lived policy template, while CompositePodGroup and PodGroup resources handle per-instance runtime scheduling state."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What's next"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about the CompositePodGroup lifecycle."},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn about the Workload API and template definitions."},{"kind":"li","text":"See how leaf groups are structured in the PodGroup API."},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about PodGroup scheduling policies."},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn about Workload-aware preemption."}],"lemmas":{"features":["feature","features"],"featuring":["featuring","feature"],"featured":["featured","feature"],"uses":["use","uses"],"used":["used","use"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"administrators":["administrator","administrators"],"wills":["will","wills"],"willing":["willing","will"],"willed":["willed","will"],"would":["would","will"],"needs":["need","needs"],"needed":["needed","need"],"needing":["needing","need"],"enables":["enables","enable"],"enabling":["enabling","enable"],"relevants":["relevants","relevant"],"components":["component","components"],"sees":["see","sees"],"seeing":["seeing","see"],"saw":["saw","see"],"seen":["seen","see"],"disables":["disables","disable"],"disabled":["disabled","disable"],"disabling":["disabling","disable"],"gates":["gate","gates"],"runtimes":["runtimes","runtime"],"objected":["objected","object"],"objecting":["objecting","object"],"represents":["represents","represent"],"represented":["represented","represent"],"representing":["representing","represent"],"nons":["nons","non"],"leaves":["leaf","leave","leaves"],"lea